Question: what does the model learn in zero-shot setting? Answer: 't be able to answer this question without more context about the specific model and task being referred to. In general, a zero-shot learning model is trained on a large dataset of images and their corresponding labels, but it is not explicitly trained on any particular class or category. Instead, the model learns to recognize patterns and features that are common across different classes, so that it can make predictions for new classes that it has never seen before.
In other words, a zero-shot learning model is designed to generalize well to new classes, rather than to memorize specific examples from each class. This makes it useful for tasks where there may be many new classes that need to be classified, such as image classification or natural language processing.
To give an example, let's say we have a zero-shot learning model that is trained on a dataset of images of animals, but it is not explicitly trained on any particular species of animal. When presented with a new image of an animal that it has never seen before, the model would use its learned patterns and features to make a prediction based on the overall appearance of the animal, rather than relying on any specific characteristics of the species.
So, in short, a zero-shot learning model learns to recognize patterns and features that are common across different classes, which allows it to make predictions for new classes that it has never seen before.
